WEBVTT

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:09.607
Good morning!

00:00:09.676 --> 00:00:10.608
Morning.

00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:11.939
Requisition reports
for the week.

00:00:12.012 --> 00:00:13.445
Thanks.

00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:16.311
What can you tell me
about the Great Wall of China?

00:00:16.383 --> 00:00:17.407
Pardon?

00:00:17.484 --> 00:00:19.918
The Great Wall of China,
on Earth. Who built it?

00:00:19.986 --> 00:00:21.283
The Chinese.

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:22.514
What for?

00:00:22.589 --> 00:00:23.613
Why does anybody build a wall?

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:24.622
To keep people out.

00:00:24.691 --> 00:00:25.680
Deck 1.

00:00:25.759 --> 00:00:28.353
In fact, it was
the first Qin emperor

00:00:28.428 --> 00:00:30.919
who connected the walls
built by a previous dynasty.

00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:31.929
He did it to prevent

00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:33.693
an invasion by nomads
to the north.

00:00:33.767 --> 00:00:35.132
How big is it?

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:36.964
I have a feeling
you're going to tell me.

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:39.971
2,400 kilometers long,
median width 3.8 meters.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.473
Before the 22nd century,

00:00:41.541 --> 00:00:43.600
it was one of the
only manmade objects

00:00:43.676 --> 00:00:45.940
that could be seen from Earth's
orbit with the naked eye.

00:00:46.012 --> 00:00:47.240
Very impressive, Neelix.

00:00:47.313 --> 00:00:49.941
I had no idea you knew so much
about my homeworld.

00:00:51.017 --> 00:00:52.609
Status?

00:00:52.685 --> 00:00:54.346
We're on a course
for the class-Y cluster.

00:00:54.421 --> 00:00:55.683
We should be there
in about three days.

00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:57.313
Gives us some time to relax.
Carry on.

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:59.756
Why this sudden interest
in the Great Wall?

00:00:59.826 --> 00:01:01.987
I've been studying
Earth landmarks.

00:01:02.062 --> 00:01:03.552
Mr. Paris and I
have been exchanging

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:05.427
a little cross-cultural trivia.

00:01:05.498 --> 00:01:07.693
He's become quite an expert
on Talaxian geography.

00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:09.530
All right, here's one for you.

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:12.366
What can you tell me
about the Millennium Gate?

00:01:12.439 --> 00:01:15.340
The Millennium Gate...
constructed in the 21st century

00:01:15.408 --> 00:01:17.137
in the United States of America.

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:18.939
It was another one
of the objects

00:01:19.045 --> 00:01:20.706
that could be seen from orbit.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:22.771
Uh, 3.2 kilometers at the base.

00:01:22.849 --> 00:01:24.407
One kilometer in height.

00:01:24.484 --> 00:01:27.453
Surface covered with
highly reflective solar panels.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:28.953
A self-contained ecosystem.

00:01:29.022 --> 00:01:31.957
It became a model
for the first colony on Mars.

00:01:32.058 --> 00:01:34.959
Did you know that one
of my ancestors built it?

00:01:35.095 --> 00:01:36.084
Really?

00:01:36.162 --> 00:01:37.891
Not with a hammer and nails,

00:01:37.964 --> 00:01:40.364
but with words
and a lot of courage.

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:43.129
Shannon O'Donnel, one of
the first woman astronauts...

00:01:43.203 --> 00:01:45.569
she was the driving force
behind the project.

00:01:45.638 --> 00:01:47.196
That's something to be proud of.

00:01:47.273 --> 00:01:50.640
We were always told stories
about her at family gatherings...

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:53.543
"The first of a long line
of Janeway explorers."

00:01:53.613 --> 00:01:55.171
Tell me more.

00:01:55.248 --> 00:01:59.207
I want something
to stump Mr. Paris with.

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:00.377
Where to begin?

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:01.545
The Millennium Gate.

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:03.452
How did she get
involved with that?

00:02:03.523 --> 00:02:05.889
Well, at that time, she was
still in the space program,

00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:08.189
but she'd also become something
of an entrepreneur.

00:02:08.261 --> 00:02:11.094
I believe she was asked
to join the project

00:02:11.164 --> 00:02:12.927
by the Governor of Indiana.

00:02:13.032 --> 00:02:16.399
He wanted her expertise on
recyclic life-support systems.

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:18.334
The way my Aunt Martha tells it,

00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:20.872
they flew her in
on a private aircraft.

00:02:25.378 --> 00:02:29.678
5:00 am, December 27, 2000.

00:02:29.749 --> 00:02:33.185
I'm in the great state
of... Indiana, I think.

00:02:33.253 --> 00:02:37.246
I saw the world's largest ball
of string this morning

00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:40.850
and the world's largest
beefsteak tomato this afternoon.

00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:42.827
It was the size
of a Volkswagen...

00:02:42.896 --> 00:02:46.093
the string, not the tomato.

00:02:46.166 --> 00:02:48.100
At least Christmas is over.

00:02:50.036 --> 00:02:52.732
Oh, no...

00:05:10.276 --> 00:05:11.368
Excuse me.

00:05:11.444 --> 00:05:13.241
I think I made a wrong turn.

00:05:13.313 --> 00:05:15.110
Is there a Millennium Gate
around here?

00:05:15.181 --> 00:05:16.113
You're looking at it.

00:05:16.182 --> 00:05:18.275
The sign said
"Gas, Food, Lodging."

00:05:18.351 --> 00:05:19.409
I need all three.

00:05:19.485 --> 00:05:21.316
You won't find any of that here.

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:24.220
Get back on the interstate
and go to Exit Nine.

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:25.814
I don't think
I'm going to make it.

00:05:25.892 --> 00:05:27.257
Good luck.

00:05:27.327 --> 00:05:31.696
Tell me, are people a little
friendlier off Exit Nine?

00:05:33.666 --> 00:05:35.133
Oh, no.

00:05:42.842 --> 00:05:44.332
You stopped.

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:45.775
You didn't.

00:05:45.845 --> 00:05:47.642
You got a pen?

00:05:47.714 --> 00:05:49.147
I... I just didn't see you.

00:05:49.215 --> 00:05:50.147
I'm sorry.

00:05:50.216 --> 00:05:51.148
Are you okay?

00:05:51.217 --> 00:05:52.343
Insurance?

00:05:52.418 --> 00:05:53.407
Not really.

00:05:53.486 --> 00:05:54.578
"Not really"?

00:05:54.654 --> 00:05:56.417
It ran out.

00:05:56.489 --> 00:05:58.457
Oh, it's not too bad.

00:05:58.524 --> 00:06:00.617
200 bucks ought to cover it.

00:06:00.693 --> 00:06:03.218
200 bucks?

00:06:03.296 --> 00:06:06.288
Let me guess...
the money ran out, too.

00:07:23.709 --> 00:07:26.610
- Hi.
- Hi.

00:07:26.679 --> 00:07:28.374
The sign said "open"?

00:07:28.448 --> 00:07:30.040
Yes, that's right.

00:07:30.116 --> 00:07:32.277
Till 6:00.

00:07:32.351 --> 00:07:34.911
Can we be of assistance?

00:07:35.021 --> 00:07:37.922
It's cold outside, and I-I
wondered if I could wait here.

00:07:38.024 --> 00:07:40.015
For what?

00:07:40.092 --> 00:07:41.150
For the tow truck.

00:07:41.227 --> 00:07:42.319
My car broke down.

00:07:42.395 --> 00:07:44.863
I'm trying to get to Florida.

00:07:44.931 --> 00:07:47.399
She doesn't look like
a corporate hit man.

00:07:47.467 --> 00:07:51.460
I assure you I'm not...
at least not anymore.

00:07:51.537 --> 00:07:56.338
Well, in any case, she's unarmed
and chilled to the bone.

00:07:57.743 --> 00:07:59.802
I think we can handle her.

00:07:59.879 --> 00:08:02.871
Zeus himself
watched over travelers.

00:08:03.015 --> 00:08:05.347
We should follow his example.

00:08:05.418 --> 00:08:06.544
Make yourself at home.

00:08:06.619 --> 00:08:07.643
Thank you.

00:08:14.093 --> 00:08:15.287
Where's your car?

00:08:15.361 --> 00:08:17.625
Down the street
next to the coffee shop...

00:08:17.697 --> 00:08:19.255
or what used to be
the coffee shop.

00:08:19.332 --> 00:08:22.233
It looks like this whole town
is closed down.

00:08:22.301 --> 00:08:23.325
It is.

00:08:23.402 --> 00:08:25.802
Except for you, huh?

00:08:25.872 --> 00:08:27.237
This ship will never sink.

00:08:27.306 --> 00:08:29.001
Right, Dad?

00:08:29.075 --> 00:08:30.201
Decaf.

00:08:32.445 --> 00:08:34.504
Not exactly
the nectar of the gods.

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:35.512
It'll do.

00:08:35.581 --> 00:08:36.673
Thank you.

00:08:39.852 --> 00:08:41.843
What's this about,
if I may ask?

00:08:41.921 --> 00:08:43.218
Preserving the past.

00:08:43.289 --> 00:08:44.847
I'm Jason, by the way.

00:08:44.924 --> 00:08:46.653
Shannon O'Donnel.

00:08:46.726 --> 00:08:49.593
I'm Henry... Henry Janeway.

00:08:49.662 --> 00:08:51.721
Hi. So, you're trying
to get them

00:08:51.797 --> 00:08:54.891
to stop building
this millennium whatever-it-is?

00:08:54.967 --> 00:08:56.832
The Millennium Gate,
the world's first

00:08:56.903 --> 00:08:58.564
self-sustaining
civic environment.

00:08:58.638 --> 00:09:00.435
That's what
the propaganda calls it.

00:09:00.506 --> 00:09:03.066
It's nothing but hype.

00:09:03.142 --> 00:09:05.508
It's actually
a glorified shopping mall.

00:09:05.578 --> 00:09:07.569
They've talked
all of my neighbors

00:09:07.647 --> 00:09:09.444
into selling their businesses.

00:09:09.515 --> 00:09:11.574
Only one thing stands
in their way.

00:09:11.651 --> 00:09:15.417
You're the last holdout?

00:09:15.488 --> 00:09:17.718
This time,
Rome withstands the barbarians.

00:09:30.303 --> 00:09:33.636
I may be able to help you
if you'd be willing to hire me.

00:09:33.706 --> 00:09:35.571
Believe me, you don't want
to be licking stamps

00:09:35.641 --> 00:09:36.573
for the next two days.

00:09:36.642 --> 00:09:38.303
We could do all this
on a computer.

00:09:38.377 --> 00:09:40.743
Yeah, but my dad doesn't
believe in computers.

00:09:40.813 --> 00:09:41.905
We don't even have one.

00:09:41.981 --> 00:09:43.209
Well, I do.

00:09:43.282 --> 00:09:45.910
We can e-mail every computer
within a hundred miles.

00:09:45.985 --> 00:09:47.009
It'll just take a few hours.

00:09:47.086 --> 00:09:48.747
It's easy.

00:09:48.821 --> 00:09:52.257
I'm not hiring anybody
right now.

00:09:52.325 --> 00:09:54.793
Besides, you're going
to Florida.

00:10:02.101 --> 00:10:06.265
Uh... maybe the tow
truck is waiting.

00:10:08.341 --> 00:10:10.434
Thanks for the coffee.

00:10:10.509 --> 00:10:11.942
You're welcome.

00:10:28.027 --> 00:10:31.485
I wouldn't ask you for a lot;
just enough to fix the car.

00:10:31.564 --> 00:10:33.828
I really...

00:10:33.899 --> 00:10:37.630
I kind of need a job right now.

00:10:37.703 --> 00:10:39.295
Dad?

00:10:42.375 --> 00:10:46.334
I suppose we could use
some help.

00:10:56.756 --> 00:10:59.850
You do believe in electricity,
don't you?

00:11:03.496 --> 00:11:04.827
Yeah!

00:11:04.897 --> 00:11:06.524
All right!

00:11:10.503 --> 00:11:13.063
So, who are these
"barbarians," anyway?

00:11:13.139 --> 00:11:14.936
A company down in Texas.

00:11:15.007 --> 00:11:16.235
Well, why come here?

00:11:16.308 --> 00:11:19.243
The city fathers
made it easy for them...

00:11:19.311 --> 00:11:21.836
free building permits,
deferred taxes, new roads...

00:11:21.914 --> 00:11:24.178
so the invaders,
they return the favor.

00:11:24.250 --> 00:11:28.448
They have offered us 20 percent
above the market price

00:11:28.521 --> 00:11:29.783
for our properties.

00:11:29.855 --> 00:11:31.322
You know what the catch is?

00:11:31.390 --> 00:11:32.880
We've all got to sell.

00:11:32.958 --> 00:11:34.255
Everybody must love you.

00:11:34.326 --> 00:11:35.725
Hmm, yeah.

00:11:35.795 --> 00:11:37.922
The people I knew
when I was growing up...

00:11:37.997 --> 00:11:38.964
family friends...

00:11:39.098 --> 00:11:40.963
it seems like they've all
just turned against me.

00:11:41.033 --> 00:11:44.298
If this were Roman times,
they'd feed me to the lions.

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:46.463
To the good old days.

00:11:46.539 --> 00:11:48.666
You know, I was born
in the wrong millennium.

00:11:48.741 --> 00:11:50.231
I'll stick with the modern age.

00:11:50.309 --> 00:11:51.241
The classical age...

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:53.210
greatest literature
mankind ever produced.

00:11:53.279 --> 00:11:54.371
No antibiotics.

00:11:54.447 --> 00:11:56.813
Families would take
care of one another.

00:11:56.882 --> 00:11:57.814
No cars.

00:11:57.883 --> 00:11:58.975
Air you can breathe.

00:11:59.118 --> 00:12:00.107
No telephones.

00:12:00.186 --> 00:12:01.312
What a pleasure.

00:12:01.387 --> 00:12:02.445
Shorter life spans.

00:12:02.521 --> 00:12:03.920
Lives that were worth living.

00:12:04.023 --> 00:12:06.048
No cold beer.

00:12:06.125 --> 00:12:08.559
There you got me.

00:12:08.627 --> 00:12:11.892
Speaking of the modern age,

00:12:11.964 --> 00:12:14.933
do you have any plans
for the Millennium Eve?

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:17.093
No different than
last year's Millennium Eve.

00:12:17.169 --> 00:12:18.101
I plan to be asleep.

00:12:18.170 --> 00:12:19.728
Life of the party.

00:12:19.805 --> 00:12:22.296
Oh, don't tell me you've bought
into all that hype again.

00:12:22.374 --> 00:12:23.534
Oh, maybe just a little.

00:12:23.609 --> 00:12:26.237
Last year, when 2000 arrived?

00:12:26.312 --> 00:12:28.678
Everyone was convinced
it was the dawn of a new era.

00:12:28.748 --> 00:12:29.840
But when the world didn't end,

00:12:29.915 --> 00:12:31.109
and the flying saucers
didn't land,

00:12:31.183 --> 00:12:34.812
and the Y2K bug didn't turn on
a single light bulb,

00:12:34.887 --> 00:12:36.218
you'd think everybody
would have realized

00:12:36.288 --> 00:12:37.255
it was a number on a calendar.

00:12:37.323 --> 00:12:38.290
But, oh, no,

00:12:38.357 --> 00:12:40.587
they had to listen
to all those hucksters

00:12:40.659 --> 00:12:42.957
who told them
the real millennium was 2001.

00:12:43.095 --> 00:12:46.087
So this New Year's Eve
will be as boring as last year.

00:12:46.165 --> 00:12:47.427
Come to think of it,

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:49.968
I have sold an awful
lot of doomsday books.

00:12:50.035 --> 00:12:51.297
See what I mean?

00:12:52.371 --> 00:12:54.601
So, what's in Florida?

00:12:54.673 --> 00:12:56.106
I have a cousin down there.

00:12:56.175 --> 00:12:59.235
I'll stay with her for a while
until I can get back on my feet.

00:12:59.311 --> 00:13:01.211
If you don't mind my asking,

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:03.214
did you lose your job?

00:13:04.583 --> 00:13:06.517
I'm in a transitional period.

00:13:06.585 --> 00:13:08.177
From what to what?

00:13:08.254 --> 00:13:11.223
From what I was doing
to what I'm going to do.

00:13:13.526 --> 00:13:15.357
Your son tells me
that your bookstore's

00:13:15.427 --> 00:13:17.395
belonged in your family
for generations...

00:13:17.463 --> 00:13:18.657
that you've never done
anything else.

00:13:18.731 --> 00:13:19.789
Yes, that's right.

00:13:19.865 --> 00:13:22.129
You know, I'm just the opposite.

00:13:22.201 --> 00:13:24.863
I love to see places
I've never been,

00:13:24.937 --> 00:13:26.165
try new things...

00:13:26.238 --> 00:13:27.933
I'm kind of an explorer.

00:13:28.073 --> 00:13:29.472
Really?

00:13:29.542 --> 00:13:31.476
Hmm. That station wagon of yours

00:13:31.544 --> 00:13:34.069
doesn't exactly look
like a sailing ship.

00:13:34.146 --> 00:13:35.670
It's a rocket ship.

00:13:35.748 --> 00:13:37.909
My mistake.

00:14:04.276 --> 00:14:05.402
We have every intention

00:14:05.477 --> 00:14:06.876
of breaking ground
on Monday,

00:14:06.946 --> 00:14:08.243
Day One
of the next millennium.

00:14:08.314 --> 00:14:09.338
The people of this town

00:14:09.415 --> 00:14:10.905
have given us
their full cooperation.

00:14:10.983 --> 00:14:12.541
That's not true!

00:14:12.618 --> 00:14:14.586
If you'll let me
finish, Mr. Janeway.

00:14:14.653 --> 00:14:16.746
With the exception
of Mr. Janeway here.

00:14:16.822 --> 00:14:18.915
Mr. Janeway, would
you like to comment?

00:14:18.991 --> 00:14:21.289
Yes. Yes, I would.

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:23.328
You-you just can't...

00:14:23.395 --> 00:14:25.158
you can't bulldoze
this town away.

00:14:25.231 --> 00:14:27.199
This-this is our heritage!
This is our past!

00:14:27.266 --> 00:14:28.733
We're trying to give
Portage Creek a future.

00:14:28.801 --> 00:14:29.768
Can't you see that?

00:14:29.835 --> 00:14:31.496
Yeah, yeah. Your future!

00:14:31.570 --> 00:14:32.798
Your future! Not ours!

00:14:33.906 --> 00:14:35.635
Mr. Moss, we've heard
that you're considering

00:14:35.708 --> 00:14:37.767
moving the project to one
of your alternate sites.

00:14:37.843 --> 00:14:39.174
Can you shed any light
on that rumor?

00:14:39.245 --> 00:14:40.576
I'm afraid that's
more than a rumor.

00:14:40.646 --> 00:14:42.273
If we can't work out an
arrangement in Portage Creek,

00:14:42.348 --> 00:14:43.838
we'll have no choice but
to select another location.

00:14:43.916 --> 00:14:44.940
What city?

00:14:45.084 --> 00:14:46.278
No comment.

00:14:46.352 --> 00:14:47.614
Is it true that Canton, Ohio,

00:14:47.686 --> 00:14:48.618
has been chosen
for the alter?

00:14:48.687 --> 00:14:49.619
Thank you for your time.

00:14:49.688 --> 00:14:50.712
Thank you, Mr. Moss.

00:14:50.789 --> 00:14:52.051
Well, that's that.

00:14:52.124 --> 00:14:54.319
All we have to do is stand
firm till New Year's.

00:14:54.393 --> 00:14:55.325
Easy for you to say.

00:14:55.394 --> 00:14:57.726
I have to go to school
with their kids.

00:14:57.796 --> 00:14:59.457
Your school is one
of the first places

00:14:59.531 --> 00:15:00.725
they're going to tear down.

00:15:00.799 --> 00:15:03.233
They said they'd build
a new one... a better one.

00:15:03.302 --> 00:15:05.236
Whose side are you on?

00:15:07.139 --> 00:15:08.629
I'm sorry.

00:15:08.707 --> 00:15:11.642
We're all in this together.

00:15:11.710 --> 00:15:12.904
Come on.

00:15:13.045 --> 00:15:15.013
Let's head back to civilization.

00:15:24.823 --> 00:15:27.291
Where shall we dine tonight?

00:15:28.928 --> 00:15:31.328
Ah, how does Paris sound?

00:15:31.397 --> 00:15:32.659
It's Friday night.

00:15:32.731 --> 00:15:34.164
Can we get a reservation?

00:15:34.233 --> 00:15:35.825
Well, I think I can arrange it.

00:15:35.901 --> 00:15:37.163
I know the maitre d'.

00:15:37.236 --> 00:15:39.170
Too bad I don't speak French.

00:15:39.238 --> 00:15:43.368
Well, we'll keep it
to ourselves.

00:15:46.745 --> 00:15:48.212
There.

00:15:50.582 --> 00:15:51.947
To new friends.

00:15:52.084 --> 00:15:53.676
And a gracious host.

00:15:53.752 --> 00:15:55.219
Bon apetit.

00:15:57.523 --> 00:15:59.787
I wouldn't mind
visiting Paris one day.

00:15:59.858 --> 00:16:01.120
Yes, it would be nice.

00:16:01.193 --> 00:16:02.455
You haven't been?

00:16:02.528 --> 00:16:04.428
I haven't been outside Indiana.

00:16:04.496 --> 00:16:06.589
These are
my traveling companions.

00:16:06.665 --> 00:16:08.792
They'll take me
anywhere anytime.

00:16:08.867 --> 00:16:10.129
It's not such a bad idea

00:16:10.202 --> 00:16:12.670
to experience the real thing
every now and then.

00:16:12.738 --> 00:16:13.830
I prefer my books.

00:16:13.906 --> 00:16:16.841
Maybe you just never
had the right guide.

00:16:16.909 --> 00:16:17.967
Is that an offer?

00:16:19.812 --> 00:16:22.212
I could make a similar argument.

00:16:22.281 --> 00:16:23.748
Not such a bad idea

00:16:23.816 --> 00:16:25.784
to settle down
every now and then.

00:16:25.851 --> 00:16:27.148
Is that an offer?

00:16:29.722 --> 00:16:32.247
You're a peculiar man,
Mr. Janeway...

00:16:32.324 --> 00:16:35.020
cloistered away
with all your books...

00:16:35.094 --> 00:16:36.925
shutting out the world...

00:16:36.996 --> 00:16:38.429
Any more peculiar than

00:16:38.497 --> 00:16:41.591
exploring the Midwest
in an ailing station wagon?

00:16:41.667 --> 00:16:43.328
Only slightly.

00:16:44.570 --> 00:16:46.367
Any word on my car?

00:16:46.438 --> 00:16:48.531
It's got a brand-new oil pan.

00:16:49.608 --> 00:16:51.337
You forgot to tell me that.

00:16:51.410 --> 00:16:54.038
Well, we were busy today.

00:16:54.113 --> 00:16:56.172
It's parked behind the garage...
whenever you're ready.

00:16:57.249 --> 00:16:59.114
First thing in the morning.

00:16:59.184 --> 00:17:00.708
Not so fast.

00:17:00.786 --> 00:17:02.845
Jason told me you promised
to show him a few tricks

00:17:02.921 --> 00:17:04.479
on the computer.

00:17:05.691 --> 00:17:08.319
I could use
another day of rest.

00:17:08.394 --> 00:17:09.452
Great.

00:17:09.528 --> 00:17:11.189
Jason'll be pleased.

00:17:12.731 --> 00:17:14.562
And when you're done with him,

00:17:14.633 --> 00:17:18.535
perhaps we'll take a stroll
along the Boulevard St. Germain.

00:17:20.706 --> 00:17:23.470
You're making it difficult
to say good-bye.

00:17:23.542 --> 00:17:26.033
Maybe that's the idea.

00:17:41.226 --> 00:17:43.160
You wished to see me, Captain?

00:17:43.228 --> 00:17:45.162
I need your help with this...

00:17:45.230 --> 00:17:46.925
The Millennium Gate.

00:17:47.066 --> 00:17:48.533
Impressive.

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:51.535
It was built by one of my
ancestors over 300 years ago.

00:17:51.603 --> 00:17:54.197
I've been digging through
the historical database,

00:17:54.273 --> 00:17:55.501
but a lot of the information

00:17:55.574 --> 00:17:58.099
from that era has
been lost or damaged.

00:17:58.177 --> 00:17:59.804
I thought you might be able

00:17:59.878 --> 00:18:02.108
to help me
reconstruct some of it.

00:18:02.181 --> 00:18:05.116
Is this relevant to
our present mission?

00:18:05.184 --> 00:18:06.776
It's relevant to me.

00:18:08.187 --> 00:18:11.588
This ancestor of yours is
15 generations removed.

00:18:11.657 --> 00:18:12.589
You only possess

00:18:12.658 --> 00:18:14.421
a small fraction
of her genetic material.

00:18:14.493 --> 00:18:15.653
Insignificant.

00:18:15.727 --> 00:18:17.592
This isn't about chromosomes,
Seven.

00:18:17.663 --> 00:18:19.255
It's about character.

00:18:20.699 --> 00:18:22.166
Explain.

00:18:24.203 --> 00:18:27.639
Shannon O'Donnel inspired me
when I was a girl.

00:18:27.706 --> 00:18:29.640
She had a...

00:18:29.708 --> 00:18:32.643
an influence
on my imagination...

00:18:32.711 --> 00:18:34.474
on my goals.

00:18:34.546 --> 00:18:35.911
I never realized

00:18:35.981 --> 00:18:39.144
genealogy could
have such an impact.

00:18:39.218 --> 00:18:41.914
I wouldn't have become
a Starfleet Captain

00:18:42.054 --> 00:18:43.487
if it wasn't for her.

00:18:44.556 --> 00:18:45.921
Temple of Artemis at Ephesus.

00:18:46.058 --> 00:18:47.320
That's four.

00:18:47.392 --> 00:18:48.654
The Colossus of Rhodes.

00:18:48.727 --> 00:18:50.160
No, in chronological order.

00:18:50.229 --> 00:18:51.491
Right. Uh...

00:18:51.563 --> 00:18:53.497
The Mausoleum of Halicarnassus.

00:18:53.565 --> 00:18:54.930
The Colossus of Rhodes.

00:18:55.067 --> 00:18:56.659
That leaves just one more.

00:18:56.735 --> 00:19:00.296
The seventh wonder of
the ancient world is...

00:19:01.673 --> 00:19:03.106
Uh-huh. Can't remember?

00:19:03.175 --> 00:19:04.142
Oh, it's right...

00:19:04.209 --> 00:19:05.870
right on the tip of my tongue.

00:19:05.978 --> 00:19:08.037
It... la...

00:19:08.113 --> 00:19:09.580
Forget it. I give up.

00:19:09.648 --> 00:19:11.275
Ha! I got you.

00:19:11.350 --> 00:19:13.113
It's the, uh...

00:19:13.185 --> 00:19:14.880
Uh...

00:19:15.020 --> 00:19:16.612
Can't remember?

00:19:16.688 --> 00:19:20.624
The Lighthouse of Alexandria
built by Ptolemy II in 280 BC.

00:19:20.692 --> 00:19:23.160
Behold the wonder
of the modern world...

00:19:23.228 --> 00:19:24.820
Borg photographic memory.

00:19:24.897 --> 00:19:26.660
We'll call this one a draw.

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:30.134
Brushing up on Earth's history?

00:19:30.202 --> 00:19:33.899
Captain Janeway requested that
I research one of her ancestors.

00:19:34.039 --> 00:19:35.472
Shannon O'Donnel.

00:19:35.541 --> 00:19:36.906
You've heard of her?

00:19:37.042 --> 00:19:38.634
Who hasn't?

00:19:38.710 --> 00:19:40.644
You mind if I lend a hand?

00:19:40.712 --> 00:19:42.179
If you wish.

00:19:45.217 --> 00:19:48.152
Sven "Buttercup" Hansen.

00:19:48.220 --> 00:19:50.245
He was one of my progenitors...

00:19:50.322 --> 00:19:52.051
a 22nd century prizefighter.

00:19:52.124 --> 00:19:53.682
The Captain encouraged me

00:19:53.759 --> 00:19:56.159
to investigate
my genealogy, as well.

00:19:56.228 --> 00:19:59.322
Well, your ancestors can
tell you a lot about yourself.

00:19:59.398 --> 00:20:02.333
Somehow, I doubt "Buttercup"
has much relevance to me.

00:20:02.401 --> 00:20:05.336
The connection could
be deeper than you think.

00:20:05.404 --> 00:20:08.168
"Sven"..."Seven."

00:20:08.240 --> 00:20:09.673
A coincidence.

00:20:09.741 --> 00:20:11.902
The point is,
there's some of him in you,

00:20:11.977 --> 00:20:14.844
just as there'll be some
of you in your descendants.

00:20:14.913 --> 00:20:16.938
If I choose to procreate.

00:20:17.082 --> 00:20:19.710
Well, I wouldn't
dismiss it so lightly.

00:20:19.785 --> 00:20:21.946
Someday, you might
enjoy a little

00:20:22.087 --> 00:20:24.851
Seven of Nine point five
running around.

00:20:24.923 --> 00:20:27.187
Or not. Or not.

00:20:27.259 --> 00:20:28.521
My research has failed.

00:20:28.594 --> 00:20:30.687
I've found no references
to this individual.

00:20:30.762 --> 00:20:33.253
Well, genealogy's
a lot like fishing...

00:20:33.332 --> 00:20:35.960
you've... you've
got to cast a wide net.

00:20:36.068 --> 00:20:38.434
Uh... computer,
expand record search

00:20:38.503 --> 00:20:40.300
for subject Shannon O'Donnel

00:20:40.372 --> 00:20:42.704
to include non-Federation
databases,

00:20:42.774 --> 00:20:44.708
personal archives,

00:20:44.776 --> 00:20:46.710
and photographic indexes,
as well.

00:20:46.778 --> 00:20:49.372
Reference found.

00:20:49.448 --> 00:20:51.507
Take a look at this.

00:20:51.583 --> 00:20:53.551
Shannon Janeway.

00:20:53.619 --> 00:20:55.985
Where in the world
did you find this?

00:20:56.121 --> 00:20:57.486
A Ferengi database.

00:20:57.556 --> 00:20:58.955
Ferengi?

00:20:59.024 --> 00:21:00.992
11 years ago,
one of their historians

00:21:01.126 --> 00:21:02.923
collected massive
amounts of data

00:21:02.995 --> 00:21:04.830
about the origin of space travel
in the Federation.

00:21:04.830 --> 00:21:06.092
About the origin of space travel
in the Federation.

00:21:06.164 --> 00:21:08.758
He wanted to market it
as a nostalgic gift item.

00:21:08.834 --> 00:21:10.631
I would have been
his first customer.

00:21:10.702 --> 00:21:13.432
It's a handsome family, Captain.

00:21:13.505 --> 00:21:15.029
These must be her sons

00:21:15.107 --> 00:21:17.632
and daughters...
grandchildren...

00:21:17.709 --> 00:21:19.176
Great-grandchildren.

00:21:19.244 --> 00:21:21.178
I've also discovered
journalistic accounts

00:21:21.246 --> 00:21:22.508
in the historical archives...

00:21:22.581 --> 00:21:24.344
mostly articles
concerning resistance

00:21:24.416 --> 00:21:25.678
to the Millennium Gate.

00:21:25.751 --> 00:21:27.946
The whole town was against her.

00:21:28.020 --> 00:21:31.285
Download this image for me,
will you, Neelix?

00:21:31.356 --> 00:21:32.880
I'm going to frame it.

00:21:32.958 --> 00:21:34.220
Yes, Captain.

00:21:34.293 --> 00:21:35.885
The citizens of Canton, Ohio,

00:21:35.961 --> 00:21:37.952
greeted Millennium Gate
representatives

00:21:38.030 --> 00:21:39.554
with a small parade today.

00:21:39.631 --> 00:21:41.098
Canton is one of three

00:21:41.166 --> 00:21:43.634
alternate sites now being
considered by Gate officials.

00:21:43.702 --> 00:21:46.500
Vowing not to bow to pressure,

00:21:46.571 --> 00:21:50.905
Henry Janeway, owner of Portage
Creek's Alexandria Books,

00:21:50.976 --> 00:21:54.139
says he has no intention
of closing down.

00:21:54.212 --> 00:21:56.237
Marci Collins,
Channel Three news.

00:21:57.716 --> 00:21:59.650
"Next stop, Mars."

00:21:59.718 --> 00:22:02.619
I used to be in
media relations with NASA.

00:22:02.688 --> 00:22:06.146
As I recall, your class
came up with that slogan.

00:22:06.224 --> 00:22:07.691
Do you mind?

00:22:11.730 --> 00:22:15.166
It's a shame
you didn't make the cut.

00:22:15.233 --> 00:22:16.666
Not good enough, I guess.

00:22:16.735 --> 00:22:19.101
Do you still keep in touch
with any of the others?

00:22:19.171 --> 00:22:20.103
No.

00:22:20.172 --> 00:22:21.935
I do. In fact, I made
a few calls last night.

00:22:22.040 --> 00:22:24.304
Remember Lieutenant McMillan?

00:22:25.377 --> 00:22:27.208
How's she doing?

00:22:27.279 --> 00:22:29.770
Copilot on a joint mission
with the Europeans

00:22:29.848 --> 00:22:31.110
scheduled for '03.

00:22:31.183 --> 00:22:32.946
Four months
on the space station.

00:22:34.019 --> 00:22:37.682
Not bad for a girl
with claustrophobia.

00:22:37.756 --> 00:22:39.189
She got over it.

00:22:39.257 --> 00:22:41.953
Sorry to hear
about your last job.

00:22:42.060 --> 00:22:44.494
All this downsizing
in aerospace...

00:22:44.563 --> 00:22:47.964
Engineers aren't given
the respect they deserve.

00:22:48.066 --> 00:22:51.160
Who told you I lost my job?

00:22:51.236 --> 00:22:52.567
I made a few calls.

00:22:52.637 --> 00:22:54.969
You don't have
to live like this...

00:22:55.073 --> 00:22:57.974
borrowing money,
sleeping in your car...

00:22:58.076 --> 00:23:00.738
You've got no business
checking up on me.

00:23:00.812 --> 00:23:03.144
Actually, I do.

00:23:03.215 --> 00:23:06.548
We run a history
on all our candidates.

00:23:06.618 --> 00:23:09.018
Are you trying to make me
some kind of an offer?

00:23:09.087 --> 00:23:12.056
We know you've been working
closely with Henry Janeway.

00:23:12.124 --> 00:23:13.557
He'll listen to you.

00:23:14.626 --> 00:23:16.116
Oh.

00:23:16.194 --> 00:23:18.162
You want me to talk to him...

00:23:18.230 --> 00:23:20.164
get him to change his mind.

00:23:20.232 --> 00:23:21.665
If you can.

00:23:21.733 --> 00:23:22.927
And in return?

00:23:23.068 --> 00:23:24.933
We'd give you a job...

00:23:25.070 --> 00:23:27.937
make you a consulting engineer
on the project.

00:23:28.039 --> 00:23:30.269
You certainly have
the credentials.

00:23:32.411 --> 00:23:33.878
Well?

00:23:36.681 --> 00:23:38.615
I'll think about it.

00:23:38.683 --> 00:23:40.514
Think quickly.

00:23:40.585 --> 00:23:43.247
We can't wait
another thousand years.

00:23:57.536 --> 00:24:01.131
Roger that and go ahead.

00:24:01.206 --> 00:24:04.733
That's one small step for man...

00:24:06.478 --> 00:24:09.675
one giant leap for mankind.

00:24:25.797 --> 00:24:27.856
- Morning.
- Morning.

00:24:27.933 --> 00:24:29.230
I didn't think you'd mind.

00:24:29.301 --> 00:24:30.893
You've got some really
great games on this thing.

00:24:30.969 --> 00:24:32.129
Don't worry about it.

00:24:32.204 --> 00:24:33.933
Have you tried Matrix of Doom?

00:24:34.039 --> 00:24:36.371
Uh, yeah. I got vaporized
on level six.

00:24:36.441 --> 00:24:39.239
Try launching a flare before
you enter the steel fortress.

00:24:39.311 --> 00:24:40.744
Cool.

00:24:40.812 --> 00:24:42.040
Coffee?

00:24:42.113 --> 00:24:43.375
Please.

00:24:43.448 --> 00:24:44.710
Where is your father?

00:24:44.783 --> 00:24:47.149
Bloomington. All the suppliers
in town are boycotting us,

00:24:47.219 --> 00:24:49.210
so he went up there
to place some orders.

00:24:49.287 --> 00:24:50.379
He left you in charge?

00:24:50.455 --> 00:24:51.752
Alexandria Books.

00:24:51.823 --> 00:24:53.188
May I help you?

00:24:53.258 --> 00:24:56.091
Sounds like you've got it down.

00:24:56.161 --> 00:24:57.958
I pay the bills, too.

00:24:58.096 --> 00:25:00.496
Dad's not very good
at accounting.

00:25:00.565 --> 00:25:01.691
You have any kids?

00:25:01.766 --> 00:25:03.028
Nope.

00:25:03.101 --> 00:25:05.092
Not a priority?

00:25:05.170 --> 00:25:06.398
I wouldn't say that.

00:25:06.471 --> 00:25:08.371
I just never got around to it.

00:25:08.440 --> 00:25:10.374
If you don't mind me asking...

00:25:10.442 --> 00:25:11.568
where's your mom?

00:25:11.643 --> 00:25:13.167
She died.

00:25:13.245 --> 00:25:16.703
Oh. I'm sorry.

00:25:16.781 --> 00:25:18.180
I was really young.

00:25:18.250 --> 00:25:20.616
I don't remember her much.

00:25:20.685 --> 00:25:23.245
Dad says none
of the great heroes

00:25:23.321 --> 00:25:25.551
grew up in a nuclear family.

00:25:25.624 --> 00:25:27.922
Hercules, Achilles, Odysseus...

00:25:28.059 --> 00:25:30.926
they all grew up
with single parents.

00:25:31.029 --> 00:25:32.519
You're in good company.

00:25:32.597 --> 00:25:34.588
You like working here, Jason?

00:25:35.800 --> 00:25:36.892
It's okay.

00:25:36.968 --> 00:25:38.526
You know, I get the feeling

00:25:38.603 --> 00:25:40.935
you don't share
your father's enthusiasm

00:25:41.006 --> 00:25:42.769
about preserving the past.

00:25:43.842 --> 00:25:44.934
I don't know.

00:25:45.010 --> 00:25:48.309
The Millennium Gate
could be pretty cool.

00:25:49.381 --> 00:25:51.941
Did you ever see the first
landing on the moon?

00:25:52.017 --> 00:25:54.076
"One small step for man..."

00:25:54.152 --> 00:25:57.212
They showed it in
science class one time.

00:25:57.289 --> 00:26:01.692
I saw it on TV
when I was 11 years old.

00:26:01.760 --> 00:26:03.421
The whole world watched it.

00:26:03.495 --> 00:26:05.360
It must have been great.

00:26:05.430 --> 00:26:06.692
Oh...

00:26:06.765 --> 00:26:07.891
it was.

00:26:08.967 --> 00:26:12.903
So, do you have any heroes?

00:26:12.971 --> 00:26:15.405
"We are not living
in a heroic age."

00:26:15.473 --> 00:26:17.168
That's what my dad says.

00:26:17.242 --> 00:26:19.733
Come on, there's
got to be somebody.

00:26:21.446 --> 00:26:22.572
Dad.

00:26:22.647 --> 00:26:24.706
I mean, he's a pain
sometimes, but...

00:26:24.783 --> 00:26:26.045
he's pretty cool.

00:26:28.787 --> 00:26:29.913
Alexandria Books.

00:26:29.988 --> 00:26:31.148
May I help you?

00:26:33.792 --> 00:26:35.089
That's all right.

00:26:37.796 --> 00:26:40.128
First call in three days
and it's a wrong number.

00:26:48.540 --> 00:26:50.303
Glorious Hector, son of Priam,

00:26:50.375 --> 00:26:52.240
slips past
the Greek front lines,

00:26:52.310 --> 00:26:55.939
bringing much-needed supplies
to the embattled city of Troy.

00:26:56.081 --> 00:26:59.608
Hey, Dad... you're famous.

00:26:59.684 --> 00:27:01.549
"Local business owner
topples Gate."

00:27:01.620 --> 00:27:02.678
Let it fall.

00:27:02.754 --> 00:27:04.051
Any customers this morning?

00:27:04.122 --> 00:27:05.555
No one here but us galliforms.

00:27:05.624 --> 00:27:08.218
That's "chickens" for the
nonscientists in the crowd.

00:27:08.293 --> 00:27:09.851
You two are dangerous together.

00:27:09.928 --> 00:27:11.759
Portage Creek will
thank us one day.

00:27:11.830 --> 00:27:14.264
You don't sacrifice history
for a shopping mall.

00:27:14.332 --> 00:27:16.197
It's a little more
than that, Henry.

00:27:16.267 --> 00:27:17.359
I beg your pardon?

00:27:17.435 --> 00:27:19.198
I said it's not just a mall.

00:27:19.270 --> 00:27:21.830
Have you read
the "promotional literature"?

00:27:21.906 --> 00:27:24.101
"Over 600 retail
spaces available.

00:27:24.175 --> 00:27:26.905
Franchise potential."

00:27:26.978 --> 00:27:28.673
There's a commercial
dimension to the project,

00:27:28.747 --> 00:27:29.873
but that's not
its only function.

00:27:29.948 --> 00:27:31.006
What's your point?

00:27:31.082 --> 00:27:34.279
I've learned a few details
about the Millennium Gate.

00:27:34.352 --> 00:27:38.755
It's a self-sustaining city,
an experimental biosphere.

00:27:38.823 --> 00:27:40.222
It's never been done before.

00:27:40.291 --> 00:27:41.417
What's wrong
with the biosphere

00:27:41.493 --> 00:27:42.425
we're living in now?

00:27:42.494 --> 00:27:44.189
- Nothing.
- Exactly.

00:27:44.262 --> 00:27:45.820
But this project
will help scientists

00:27:45.897 --> 00:27:47.364
learn more about
our environment...

00:27:47.432 --> 00:27:49.263
possibly even recreate it
on other worlds.

00:27:49.334 --> 00:27:50.596
Other worlds?

00:27:50.669 --> 00:27:52.432
Don't we have enough problems
on the one we're on?

00:27:52.504 --> 00:27:55.735
Yeah. Which is why I'd like
to get the hell off it one day.

00:27:55.807 --> 00:27:58.503
I think you're taking
this exploring a little too far.

00:27:58.576 --> 00:28:00.168
And you're so afraid of change,

00:28:00.245 --> 00:28:02.440
you can barely walk
out that front door.

00:28:04.482 --> 00:28:07.007
The Millennium Gate
has a lot of potential, Henry.

00:28:07.085 --> 00:28:08.177
You just can't see it.

00:28:08.253 --> 00:28:09.811
All you can see is these books.

00:28:09.888 --> 00:28:11.219
You're living in the past.

00:28:11.289 --> 00:28:14.224
Sounds like you've been
sleeping with the enemy.

00:28:14.292 --> 00:28:16.954
Having a beer, anyway.

00:28:18.029 --> 00:28:19.097
Moss.

00:28:19.164 --> 00:28:20.563
He offered me a job.

00:28:20.632 --> 00:28:21.690
Doing what?

00:28:21.766 --> 00:28:23.199
Consulting engineer.

00:28:23.268 --> 00:28:24.860
On the Millennium Gate.

00:28:24.936 --> 00:28:26.460
I said I'd think about it.

00:28:26.538 --> 00:28:27.664
And he said, "Get Janeway

00:28:27.739 --> 00:28:29.730
to sign on the dotted
line and you're hired."

00:28:29.808 --> 00:28:31.901
That's one way of looking at it.

00:28:31.976 --> 00:28:33.603
And when I say no, then what?

00:28:33.678 --> 00:28:35.669
You're going to go with them,
right?

00:28:35.747 --> 00:28:38.215
To Canton, Ohio,
or the Third Circle of Hell

00:28:38.283 --> 00:28:41.411
or wherever it is they decide
to build this damn thing.

00:28:41.486 --> 00:28:43.386
I can't keep living
out of my car.

00:28:43.455 --> 00:28:44.581
You don't have to.

00:28:44.656 --> 00:28:45.714
- Stay here.
- No.

00:28:45.790 --> 00:28:47.223
- Why not?
- Because I can't.

00:28:47.292 --> 00:28:48.520
That's not a reason.

00:28:48.593 --> 00:28:50.959
Because I don't want to.

00:28:52.130 --> 00:28:54.030
You've been really
nice to me, Henry,

00:28:54.099 --> 00:28:55.566
but I can't get stuck here.

00:28:55.633 --> 00:28:56.930
"Stuck"?

00:28:57.068 --> 00:28:58.296
Like me?

00:28:58.369 --> 00:28:59.358
I didn't say that.

00:28:59.437 --> 00:29:01.268
You can live
however you want to.

00:29:01.339 --> 00:29:02.567
And so can you.

00:29:06.911 --> 00:29:08.173
What's going on?

00:29:08.246 --> 00:29:10.544
We're helping our guest
resume her journey.

00:29:10.615 --> 00:29:13.243
But I thought you were
staying here for a while.

00:29:13.318 --> 00:29:14.410
Something came up.

00:29:14.486 --> 00:29:15.612
We can manage by ourselves.

00:29:15.687 --> 00:29:18.747
I guess saying good-bye isn't
that difficult after all.

00:29:29.100 --> 00:29:31.364
Jason.

00:29:31.436 --> 00:29:33.370
Jason, where you going?

00:29:33.438 --> 00:29:34.700
Aunt Pat's.

00:29:34.773 --> 00:29:36.741
Happy New Year.

00:29:44.783 --> 00:29:46.080
Jason!

00:29:48.019 --> 00:29:49.486
Jason!

00:29:58.062 --> 00:29:59.188
It was around 2210.

00:29:59.264 --> 00:30:01.732
My Uncle Jack was
on a deep space mission

00:30:01.800 --> 00:30:03.028
to Beta Capricus.

00:30:03.101 --> 00:30:06.070
That's when "deep space"
meant the next star over.

00:30:06.137 --> 00:30:08.935
And that was when they still
had to go into stasis.

00:30:09.040 --> 00:30:12.032
So Jack put his crew under
as soon as they left orbit

00:30:12.110 --> 00:30:14.738
and piloted the ship
by himself for six months.

00:30:14.813 --> 00:30:17.145
No contact with anybody
along the way?

00:30:17.215 --> 00:30:18.739
There wasn't anybody
along the way.

00:30:18.817 --> 00:30:19.841
Not back then.

00:30:19.918 --> 00:30:21.476
The transmitter
wasn't even subspace.

00:30:21.553 --> 00:30:24.113
It took weeks to get
a message back to Earth.

00:30:24.189 --> 00:30:25.349
I would prefer stasis.

00:30:25.423 --> 00:30:26.947
Me, too.
That long alone,

00:30:27.091 --> 00:30:28.956
I'd probably go a little batty.

00:30:29.060 --> 00:30:30.960
So, six months to Beta Capricus

00:30:31.062 --> 00:30:34.463
and when they finally arrive...
there's nothing there.

00:30:34.532 --> 00:30:36.966
No planets?

00:30:37.101 --> 00:30:38.625
No. No star, no nothing.

00:30:38.703 --> 00:30:41.831
It turns out Beta Capricus
was just an EM echo

00:30:41.906 --> 00:30:42.964
of a distant galaxy.

00:30:43.107 --> 00:30:44.836
What was his course of action?

00:30:44.909 --> 00:30:45.967
What else could he do?

00:30:46.077 --> 00:30:48.170
He turned the ship around
and headed home.

00:30:48.246 --> 00:30:49.975
And the crew?

00:30:50.081 --> 00:30:52.675
He figured there was
no reason to bother them.

00:30:52.750 --> 00:30:54.945
There's nothing to see,
nothing to do,

00:30:55.019 --> 00:30:57.453
so six months later
he gets back to Earth,

00:30:57.522 --> 00:30:59.285
brings everybody out of stasis

00:30:59.357 --> 00:31:02.724
and they wake up wondering
why they haven't left orbit.

00:31:03.862 --> 00:31:04.851
Come in.

00:31:05.997 --> 00:31:09.125
Oh. I didn't realize
you were in a briefing.

00:31:09.200 --> 00:31:10.428
Not at all.

00:31:10.501 --> 00:31:12.594
We're talking
about our family histories.

00:31:12.670 --> 00:31:14.160
Oh. This can wait.

00:31:14.239 --> 00:31:15.797
May I... join you?

00:31:15.874 --> 00:31:17.205
Please.

00:31:17.275 --> 00:31:19.937
I, too, come
from a distinguished line.

00:31:20.078 --> 00:31:22.308
His cousin's an electric shaver.

00:31:23.448 --> 00:31:24.380
Hardly.

00:31:24.449 --> 00:31:27.316
My program was compiled
from the most advanced

00:31:27.385 --> 00:31:29.376
holo-matrices in the Federation.

00:31:29.454 --> 00:31:32.218
My cousin was
a prize-winning chess program.

00:31:32.290 --> 00:31:33.416
Ensign Paris,

00:31:33.491 --> 00:31:35.959
you have yet to elaborate
on your family origins.

00:31:36.027 --> 00:31:39.258
Well, they were
a pretty ordinary bunch,

00:31:39.330 --> 00:31:41.764
salt-of-the-earth type people.

00:31:41.833 --> 00:31:45.269
Farmers, mostly,
some planetary colonists...

00:31:45.336 --> 00:31:48.237
but there was one...
he was a pilot.

00:31:48.306 --> 00:31:52.299
He flew the first orbital glider
over the lower Martian plateau.

00:31:52.377 --> 00:31:53.674
Mars.

00:31:53.745 --> 00:31:56.305
Your ancestor must
have known the Captain's.

00:31:56.381 --> 00:31:59.179
She did work
on all the early Mars projects.

00:31:59.250 --> 00:32:01.582
Looks like we go way back,
Mr. Paris.

00:32:01.653 --> 00:32:02.779
What was her name?

00:32:02.854 --> 00:32:04.412
Shannon O'Donnel.

00:32:04.489 --> 00:32:06.423
O'Donnel...

00:32:06.491 --> 00:32:08.425
Um...

00:32:08.493 --> 00:32:10.427
I don't think so.

00:32:10.495 --> 00:32:11.894
What do you mean?

00:32:11.963 --> 00:32:15.626
Well, I know all the Mars
projects from the 1970s on...

00:32:15.700 --> 00:32:18.260
unmanned, manned, who's who...

00:32:18.336 --> 00:32:21.305
there were no O'Donnels
in any of them.

00:32:31.282 --> 00:32:32.613
Come in.

00:32:32.684 --> 00:32:33.946
Ship's status report.

00:32:34.018 --> 00:32:35.417
Let me guess.

00:32:35.486 --> 00:32:36.817
The holographic engineer

00:32:36.888 --> 00:32:38.913
is having problems
with her program;

00:32:38.990 --> 00:32:41.959
Neelix, the Cardassian
cook, is low on supplies;

00:32:42.093 --> 00:32:44.254
Seven of 12 is regenerating

00:32:44.329 --> 00:32:47.696
and Captain Chakotay
is doing just fine.

00:32:47.765 --> 00:32:50.359
Just wondering how they'll
piece together our lives

00:32:50.435 --> 00:32:51.925
a few hundred years from now.

00:32:52.003 --> 00:32:53.664
Depends on
how big the pieces are.

00:32:53.738 --> 00:32:55.535
A padd here,
a Captain's log there,

00:32:55.606 --> 00:32:57.506
maybe a couple
of holodeck programs.

00:32:57.575 --> 00:33:00.476
It won't be as much to
go on as we might think.

00:33:00.545 --> 00:33:02.604
I've gone through
dozens of histories

00:33:02.680 --> 00:33:04.409
written about
21st century Earth.

00:33:04.482 --> 00:33:07.042
All of them biased
in one way or another.

00:33:07.118 --> 00:33:09.245
The Vulcans describe
first contact

00:33:09.320 --> 00:33:11.515
with a "savagely
illogical" race.

00:33:11.589 --> 00:33:15.116
Ferengi talk about Wall Street
as if it were holy ground.

00:33:15.193 --> 00:33:16.854
The Bolians express dismay

00:33:16.928 --> 00:33:19.726
at the low quality
of human plumbing.

00:33:19.797 --> 00:33:22.595
And human historians...
exact same story.

00:33:22.667 --> 00:33:24.760
Every culture saw it
a different way.

00:33:24.836 --> 00:33:26.565
So I go back
to the raw material...

00:33:26.637 --> 00:33:28.628
birth certificates,
death certificates

00:33:28.706 --> 00:33:30.640
marriage certificates,
census surveys,

00:33:30.708 --> 00:33:32.938
voter registration forms,
housing records,

00:33:33.044 --> 00:33:35.535
medical, employment...
court records.

00:33:35.613 --> 00:33:37.672
It's all fragmented
and incomplete.

00:33:37.749 --> 00:33:41.150
So... did she exist?

00:33:41.219 --> 00:33:43.949
Her name was Shannon O'Donnel.

00:33:44.022 --> 00:33:47.082
She did train to be
an astronaut...

00:33:47.158 --> 00:33:49.092
but she didn't finish.

00:33:49.160 --> 00:33:51.094
She was an engineer...

00:33:51.162 --> 00:33:53.756
but never worked on
the Mars missions.

00:33:53.831 --> 00:33:55.731
Did she work
on the Millennium Gate?

00:33:56.801 --> 00:33:58.769
Only as a consultant.

00:33:58.836 --> 00:34:00.963
What about all the opposition
you spoke of?

00:34:01.072 --> 00:34:03.563
You said she fought
to get the project underway.

00:34:03.641 --> 00:34:05.632
There was no opposition.

00:34:05.710 --> 00:34:09.077
In fact, the Millennium Gate
was greeted with open arms

00:34:09.147 --> 00:34:11.877
by the local population...

00:34:11.949 --> 00:34:15.043
except for one man.

00:34:15.119 --> 00:34:16.780
"Henry Janeway."

00:34:16.854 --> 00:34:20.119
She married him...
and changed her name,

00:34:20.191 --> 00:34:23.058
but she certainly
never changed history.

00:34:23.127 --> 00:34:24.458
Don't be too hard on her.

00:34:24.529 --> 00:34:25.894
She may not have known

00:34:25.963 --> 00:34:28.363
she was supposed to live up
to your expectations.

00:34:28.433 --> 00:34:29.730
Oh, I'll get over it.

00:34:29.801 --> 00:34:32.929
But the question is:
When we get back to Earth,

00:34:33.004 --> 00:34:35.996
how will I break the news
to Aunt Martha?

00:34:38.109 --> 00:34:39.599
With less
than three hours to go,

00:34:39.677 --> 00:34:41.645
Henry Janeway still refuses
to cooperate

00:34:41.712 --> 00:34:43.543
with Millennium Gate
representatives.

00:34:43.614 --> 00:34:47.277
I'll return with live updates
as midnight approaches.

00:34:47.351 --> 00:34:49.546
Marci Collins,
Channel Three news.

00:34:49.620 --> 00:34:51.952
This has been
a Channel Three Special Report.

00:34:52.023 --> 00:34:54.423
I don't suppose there's any
chance he'll change his mind.

00:34:54.492 --> 00:34:56.483
- By midnight? Doubtful.
- Then it looks like

00:34:56.561 --> 00:34:58.051
we'll have to scrub the launch.

00:34:58.129 --> 00:35:00.597
At least where Portage Creek
is concerned.

00:35:00.665 --> 00:35:03.896
It's a shame how one ignorant
man can stand in the way

00:35:03.968 --> 00:35:04.900
of progress.

00:35:04.969 --> 00:35:06.129
It's not that simple.

00:35:06.204 --> 00:35:08.138
Henry's a very likable guy,

00:35:08.206 --> 00:35:10.470
but he's playing
for the wrong team.

00:35:10.541 --> 00:35:11.906
He's looking back.

00:35:11.976 --> 00:35:14.809
This town needs people who
look towards the future.

00:35:14.879 --> 00:35:16.471
Well, while you're
looking forward,

00:35:16.547 --> 00:35:17.844
I'll be driving to Florida.

00:35:17.915 --> 00:35:19.542
Wait a minute.

00:35:19.617 --> 00:35:21.312
I'm not about to turn
a fellow explorer

00:35:21.385 --> 00:35:22.317
out into the cold.

00:35:22.386 --> 00:35:24.377
I didn't hold up
my end of the bargain.

00:35:24.455 --> 00:35:25.387
If we didn't think

00:35:25.456 --> 00:35:27.583
you had something
to add to this project,

00:35:27.658 --> 00:35:30.525
we never would have made
the offer in the first place.

00:35:30.595 --> 00:35:33.496
Henry Janeway's pigheadedness
doesn't change that.

00:35:33.564 --> 00:35:35.327
We could still use your help...

00:35:35.399 --> 00:35:37.333
just not here.

00:35:37.401 --> 00:35:39.198
My associate in Canton.

00:35:39.270 --> 00:35:41.170
I'll tell him you're on the way.

00:35:41.239 --> 00:35:42.934
He'll find a place for you.

00:35:43.007 --> 00:35:44.375
Shannon!

00:35:44.442 --> 00:35:46.842
What's the matter?

00:35:46.911 --> 00:35:48.640
It's Dad.

00:35:48.713 --> 00:35:50.772
See you in Ohio?

00:35:50.848 --> 00:35:52.213
He won't leave the shop.

00:35:52.283 --> 00:35:53.944
There's all these people
outside.

00:35:54.085 --> 00:35:55.313
The police are there.

00:35:55.386 --> 00:35:57.946
Your father can
take care of himself.

00:35:58.055 --> 00:35:58.953
Please, won't you talk to him?

00:35:59.090 --> 00:35:59.954
He'll listen to you.

00:36:00.091 --> 00:36:01.353
No, he doesn't, Jason.

00:36:01.425 --> 00:36:03.086
Where are you going?

00:36:03.161 --> 00:36:04.753
And what's this about
"I'll see you in Ohio"?

00:36:04.829 --> 00:36:06.319
Ohio... Florida...

00:36:06.397 --> 00:36:07.830
What's wrong with this place?

00:36:07.899 --> 00:36:08.888
It didn't work out.

00:36:09.000 --> 00:36:10.092
I thought you liked us.

00:36:10.168 --> 00:36:11.362
I do.

00:36:11.435 --> 00:36:13.027
Then why are you leaving?

00:36:13.104 --> 00:36:14.503
I'm sorry.

00:36:19.210 --> 00:36:23.442
December 31, 2000.
11:15 p.m.

00:36:23.514 --> 00:36:26.312
I've got 95 miles of interstate

00:36:26.384 --> 00:36:29.410
before I have to decide
whether I head east or south,

00:36:29.487 --> 00:36:32.217
but those 95 miles
won't be uneventful.

00:36:32.290 --> 00:36:34.918
My guidebook tells me
that I'm not too far

00:36:35.026 --> 00:36:37.654
from Leonardo da Vinci's
Last Supper...

00:36:37.728 --> 00:36:40.356
re-created entirely in corn.

00:36:47.572 --> 00:36:51.372
The last few days have been
memorable, to say the least.

00:36:51.442 --> 00:36:54.138
I met Henry Janeway.

00:36:54.212 --> 00:36:55.645
Interesting man.

00:36:55.713 --> 00:36:57.476
Liked to talk.

00:36:57.548 --> 00:37:00.915
Unfortunately, he doesn't listen
to anybody but himself.

00:37:00.985 --> 00:37:04.148
He gave me a place
to stay, though...

00:37:04.222 --> 00:37:06.383
and we had dinner...

00:37:06.457 --> 00:37:09.392
in Paris, no less.

00:37:09.460 --> 00:37:12.054
He has a son.

00:37:12.129 --> 00:37:14.529
Good kid.

00:37:14.599 --> 00:37:16.726
Bright, like his father.

00:37:38.055 --> 00:37:39.818
I personally don't regret
a single day

00:37:39.890 --> 00:37:41.118
I've spent in this lovely town,

00:37:41.192 --> 00:37:42.318
and I want to thank you all

00:37:42.393 --> 00:37:43.860
for your encouragement
and support.

00:37:43.928 --> 00:37:45.919
Would you consider
extending your deadline?

00:37:46.063 --> 00:37:47.360
I'm afraid that's not possible.

00:37:47.431 --> 00:37:49.092
We'd like to express
our deepest regret

00:37:49.166 --> 00:37:51.566
to the town of Portage Creek
and on behalf of...

00:37:51.636 --> 00:37:52.625
Excuse me.

00:37:52.703 --> 00:37:53.692
Get this.

00:37:55.573 --> 00:37:57.541
Let her through.

00:38:13.791 --> 00:38:16.259
We're closed.

00:38:16.327 --> 00:38:17.817
It's me.

00:38:21.565 --> 00:38:23.260
I said, we're closed.

00:38:23.334 --> 00:38:25.427
That's not what the sign says.

00:38:30.408 --> 00:38:33.070
Damn it, Henry,
it's cold out here.

00:38:34.145 --> 00:38:35.578
Who's with you?

00:38:35.646 --> 00:38:37.841
Nobody but us galliforms.

00:38:43.988 --> 00:38:46.149
Thought you'd be in Ohio by now.

00:38:46.223 --> 00:38:47.713
Car break down again?

00:38:47.792 --> 00:38:49.623
What the hell are you doing?

00:38:49.694 --> 00:38:51.753
Get your things.
We're leaving.

00:38:51.829 --> 00:38:53.763
I'm not going anywhere.

00:38:53.831 --> 00:38:55.765
Do I have to drag you
out the front door?

00:38:55.833 --> 00:38:56.891
- You can try.
- Henry...

00:38:56.967 --> 00:38:58.264
- I won't leave.
- It's over.

00:38:58.336 --> 00:38:59.928
Well, then I'm going to have
to ask you to leave.

00:39:00.004 --> 00:39:00.936
Half the town is out there.

00:39:01.005 --> 00:39:01.937
You're on the news.

00:39:02.006 --> 00:39:03.200
Maybe you'll even go
down in history.

00:39:03.274 --> 00:39:04.206
You have made your point.

00:39:04.275 --> 00:39:05.765
Obviously, I haven't,
because if I had,

00:39:05.843 --> 00:39:07.743
you wouldn't be asking me
to sign my life away.

00:39:07.812 --> 00:39:09.109
Maybe if you'd looked up
from your books

00:39:09.180 --> 00:39:11.307
once in a while, you'd see
what's in front of you.

00:39:11.382 --> 00:39:13.009
Oh, please, no more
speeches about the future.

00:39:13.084 --> 00:39:14.278
As long as I don't
have to hear you

00:39:14.352 --> 00:39:15.284
pontificate about the past.

00:39:15.353 --> 00:39:16.285
It's a deal.

00:39:16.354 --> 00:39:18.117
Glad we had this chat.
Good-bye.

00:39:18.189 --> 00:39:19.451
It's almost midnight, Henry.

00:39:19.523 --> 00:39:20.717
Exactly. I've won.

00:39:20.791 --> 00:39:21.723
What have you won?

00:39:21.792 --> 00:39:23.020
The right to hide
behind these shelves

00:39:23.094 --> 00:39:24.026
for the rest of your life?

00:39:24.095 --> 00:39:25.027
It's worked so far.

00:39:25.096 --> 00:39:26.688
Well, it isn't working
for your son...

00:39:26.764 --> 00:39:29.494
or this town...
or me.

00:39:29.567 --> 00:39:31.262
Look, I know
you've got a job to do.

00:39:31.335 --> 00:39:32.529
Oh, this isn't about the job.

00:39:32.603 --> 00:39:33.763
Then why are you here?

00:39:35.439 --> 00:39:37.100
It was the cookies.

00:39:37.174 --> 00:39:39.005
"Cookies"?

00:39:41.345 --> 00:39:43.506
I was on the interstate.

00:39:43.581 --> 00:39:45.105
I stopped for gas

00:39:45.182 --> 00:39:48.015
and I bought a bag of
chocolate chip cookies

00:39:48.085 --> 00:39:49.575
at a convenience store.

00:39:49.653 --> 00:39:51.348
It's a little ritual of mine.

00:39:51.422 --> 00:39:53.014
Whenever I get back
on the highway,

00:39:53.090 --> 00:39:54.182
I like to treat myself.

00:39:54.258 --> 00:39:56.886
I see.

00:39:56.961 --> 00:40:02.331
Well, uh... what does this
have to do with anything?

00:40:06.370 --> 00:40:09.168
They didn't taste good, Henry.

00:40:09.240 --> 00:40:11.174
It wasn't the same.

00:40:12.910 --> 00:40:15.208
I just kept thinking about you

00:40:15.279 --> 00:40:17.770
and how I wish you'd been there.

00:40:18.849 --> 00:40:21.477
Actually,
I prefer oatmeal cookies.

00:40:21.552 --> 00:40:23.383
I'm allergic to chocolate.

00:40:23.454 --> 00:40:25.285
Oh, you don't know
what you're missing.

00:40:27.858 --> 00:40:30.486
It's been a long time...

00:40:30.561 --> 00:40:32.085
but I'm starting to feel

00:40:32.163 --> 00:40:34.461
like maybe
I found a place where...

00:40:37.334 --> 00:40:39.427
I'd like to stick around
for a while,

00:40:39.503 --> 00:40:41.403
with you and Jason.

00:40:41.472 --> 00:40:43.940
And we'd like to have you.

00:40:44.008 --> 00:40:47.205
But I can't work in a bookstore
for the rest of my life.

00:40:47.278 --> 00:40:50.270
I've been given
a second chance, Henry,

00:40:50.347 --> 00:40:52.076
and I can't lose that.

00:40:52.149 --> 00:40:54.413
I'm stuck in the future.

00:40:54.485 --> 00:40:55.850
You're stuck in the past.

00:40:55.920 --> 00:41:00.289
But maybe we could... get
unstuck in the present.

00:41:02.927 --> 00:41:05.327
I... I don't see how

00:41:05.396 --> 00:41:07.921
with-without sacrificing my...

00:41:08.032 --> 00:41:09.329
You know, Mr. Moss offered me

00:41:09.400 --> 00:41:11.095
a job in Canton,
even though I failed

00:41:11.168 --> 00:41:12.897
to keep up my end
of the bargain.

00:41:12.970 --> 00:41:14.597
I guess he felt sorry for me.

00:41:15.673 --> 00:41:19.575
But I'm prepared
to turn him down...

00:41:19.643 --> 00:41:21.235
if you want me to stay with you.

00:41:27.418 --> 00:41:32.151
I suppose I could...
reopen my shop

00:41:32.223 --> 00:41:34.783
in that monstrosity
you want to build.

00:41:36.494 --> 00:41:38.621
Well, I'll have
a few connections.

00:41:38.696 --> 00:41:41.859
I could probably get you
a nice remote location

00:41:41.932 --> 00:41:43.866
so nobody would bother you.

00:41:43.934 --> 00:41:45.492
Not much profit in that.

00:41:46.904 --> 00:41:49.270
You sure you won't
drive away again?

00:41:50.341 --> 00:41:51.899
No.

00:41:51.976 --> 00:41:56.470
But if I do, we'll make
the trip together.

00:41:57.548 --> 00:41:58.947
What time is it?

00:42:00.017 --> 00:42:01.484
A minute to midnight.

00:42:01.552 --> 00:42:03.144
Then we're not too late.

00:42:48.732 --> 00:42:51.223
Neelix to Captain Janeway.

00:42:54.772 --> 00:42:56.501
Yes?

00:42:56.574 --> 00:42:59.372
Captain, would you mind
coming to the Mess Hall?

00:42:59.443 --> 00:43:00.808
Is there a problem?

00:43:00.878 --> 00:43:02.140
No, no emergency,

00:43:02.212 --> 00:43:04.373
but I need to speak with you.

00:43:04.448 --> 00:43:05.779
A personal matter.

00:43:05.849 --> 00:43:08.215
Give me a minute.

00:43:11.221 --> 00:43:15.851
- Happy Ancestor's Eve!
- Happy Ancestor's Eve!

00:43:15.926 --> 00:43:17.518
What's all this?

00:43:17.595 --> 00:43:19.586
It's April 22, Ancestor's Eve.

00:43:19.663 --> 00:43:22.063
It's a holiday
first established...

00:43:22.132 --> 00:43:23.861
Well, uh, today actually

00:43:23.934 --> 00:43:26.061
with the Captain's permission.

00:43:26.136 --> 00:43:27.626
Neelix...

00:43:27.705 --> 00:43:29.434
I think he's
onto something, Captain.

00:43:29.506 --> 00:43:30.734
An evening of reflection

00:43:30.808 --> 00:43:32.105
in honor of those
who came before.

00:43:32.176 --> 00:43:33.108
Here, here.

00:43:33.177 --> 00:43:34.166
Uncle Jack would approve.

00:43:34.244 --> 00:43:35.768
It got me out from
under a warp conduit.

00:43:35.846 --> 00:43:36.778
I'm all for it.

00:43:36.847 --> 00:43:38.940
I appreciate what you're
trying to do, but...

00:43:39.016 --> 00:43:41.041
Neelix, the gift.

00:43:41.118 --> 00:43:42.779
What gift?

00:43:44.054 --> 00:43:49.185
Shannon O'Donnel Janeway,
circa 2050.

00:43:49.259 --> 00:43:50.886
We did a little more research.

00:43:50.961 --> 00:43:53.555
This photograph
was taken in a small park

00:43:53.631 --> 00:43:55.963
near Portage Creek,
38 years after

00:43:56.033 --> 00:43:57.728
the dedication
of the Millennium Gate.

00:43:57.801 --> 00:44:00.702
I thought it would look
nice in your ready room

00:44:00.771 --> 00:44:02.568
on the shelf next to your desk.

00:44:02.640 --> 00:44:03.902
Thank you.

00:44:04.008 --> 00:44:06.636
But I'm not so sure she
has a place there anymore.

00:44:06.710 --> 00:44:08.075
You are mistaken, Captain.

00:44:08.145 --> 00:44:09.077
Oh?

00:44:09.146 --> 00:44:11.307
Her life captured
your imagination.

00:44:11.382 --> 00:44:13.350
Historical details
are irrelevant.

00:44:13.417 --> 00:44:15.282
I concur with that analysis.

00:44:15.352 --> 00:44:17.013
If it weren't
for Shannon O'Donnel,

00:44:17.087 --> 00:44:18.281
you never would have
joined Starfleet.

00:44:18.355 --> 00:44:19.754
Yeah, and I would have never

00:44:19.823 --> 00:44:21.723
got you all stuck here
in the Delta Quadrant.

00:44:21.792 --> 00:44:23.783
It gave us all time
to get to know each other.

00:44:23.861 --> 00:44:25.954
Time for a family portrait
of our own.

00:44:28.065 --> 00:44:31.466
Everyone gather 'round
the Captain, please.

00:44:33.504 --> 00:44:36.064
Face the camera.

00:44:36.140 --> 00:44:37.767
Smile.

00:44:38.809 --> 00:44:42.245
- To family.
- To family.

00:44:42.312 --> 00:44:43.506
Another one for posterity.

00:44:43.580 --> 00:44:44.512
No, no.

00:44:44.581 --> 00:44:46.515
I think we should
get out of here.

00:44:46.583 --> 00:44:47.515
I don't think so.

00:44:47.584 --> 00:44:48.551
Enough is enough.

00:44:54.291 --> 00:44:55.918
Knock it off, Kieran.

00:44:55.993 --> 00:44:56.982
That's an order.
